# ─── Console ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your UniFi OS console: a UDM / UDM Pro / UDM SE, a UNVR, or a Cloud Key Gen2+.
# The IP or hostname is enough — https:// is assumed, and an explicit :port is
# preserved. Anything after the host is discarded, so pasting a browser URL works.
UNIFI_PROTECT_HOST=192.168.1.1

# ─── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A console login. Create a DEDICATED LOCAL user rather than reusing your own:
#   UniFi OS → Settings → Admins & Users → Add User → Local Access Only
# Two reasons. A Ubiquiti cloud (SSO) account often cannot log in locally at all,
# and a local account scoped to Protect keeps the blast radius off the rest of
# the console. Give it View Only unless you intend to enable writes below.
UNIFI_PROTECT_USERNAME=mcp
UNIFI_PROTECT_PASSWORD=

# Only needed if the account has two-factor enabled — and it is close to useless
# here, because a TOTP code expires in about 30 seconds and this is read once at
# startup. Prefer calling the unifi_protect_auth_login tool with a current code:
# the session it establishes is cached and reused, so it is a one-off.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_TOTP=

# ─── TLS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Certificate verification is ON by default, and turning it off is scoped to
# THIS server's requests through a dedicated undici dispatcher — not the whole
# process, and not NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED.
#
# Verifying needs TWO things together, and either alone achieves nothing:
#
#   1. The certificate is self-signed, so the machine must be told to trust it:
#        openssl s_client -showcerts -connect <console>:443 </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
#          | openssl x509 > ~/.config/unifi-protect/console.pem
#        export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=~/.config/unifi-protect/console.pem
#   2. The certificate is issued to `unifi.local`, with SANs for unifi.local,
#      localhost and 127.0.0.1 — and NO IP SAN. So UNIFI_PROTECT_HOST must be a
#      host name that resolves to the console. Reached by IP, verification fails
#      on the host name no matter how the certificate is trusted. (Measured:
#      `curl --cacert` gives ssl_verify=0 by name, ssl_verify=1 by IP.)
#
# If the console has no DNS or mDNS name on your network, add one — a hosts
# entry is enough — or accept the unverified connection below. The startup
# banner prints tls=UNVERIFIED on every run so this is never silently forgotten.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_VERIFY_TLS=false

# ─── Safety ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default. When off, the mutating tools are NOT REGISTERED — they do not
# appear in tools/list and cannot be called at all, rather than being refused.
#
# Setting this to 1 unlocks 12 tools: renaming and reconfiguring cameras, lights,
# sensors, viewers and chimes; changing recording mode (including `never`, which
# stops footage being kept); PTZ movement; console-wide settings; and rebooting
# a camera or the console itself. The two reboots additionally require an
# explicit confirm argument.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_ALLOW_WRITES=1

# ─── Files ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the session cookie is cached, written with mode 600. Defaults to
# ~/.config/unifi-protect/session.json.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_SESSION_FILE=

# Where snapshots, event thumbnails and video exports are written by default.
# Defaults to ~/.cache/unifi-protect.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_SNAPSHOT_DIR=

# An alternative config file. Defaults to ~/.config/unifi-protect/config.json.
# Environment variables always win over the file, field by field.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_CONFIG=

# ─── Tuning ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Retries per request, for 401 (re-login), 429 and 5xx. Default 3.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_MAX_RETRIES=3

# Refuse any single download larger than this, so an over-broad video export
# fails with a clear message instead of exhausting memory. Default 200 MB.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES=200000000

# How long the camera id→name index is cached, in seconds. Default 60.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_DEVICE_CACHE_TTL=60

# Verbose request logging to stderr.
# UNIFI_PROTECT_DEBUG=1
